Dominic Raab: The government cannot apologize for the decision to isolate Spain
Dominic Raab defended the government's sudden decision to reimpose isolation measures on Spain.
Ministers announced on Saturday that 14 days of self-isolation would be required if holidaymakers could not return from Spain and its islands before midnight.
Spain has been on the list of safe travel corridors on Downing Street, however, the recent surge in coronavirus cases has raised public concerns about the second wave of the outbreak.
Defending the sudden re-imposition of travel restrictions on Sunday, the Foreign Minister said the move was a real-time response to the surge in reported cases of infection in the region.
He said that the situation had been assessed the previous afternoon and that we had made a decision as soon as possible.
He added: "Oh, we can't apologize for this.
We must be able to act quickly and decisively, especially when it comes to the country or Spain and specific countries.
Otherwise, the UK will face another risk of infection, triggering a second wave of outbreaks, and then re-impose a lockdown.
Transport ministers face mandatory quarantine, as do holidaymakers who travel to Spain
The government made the decision after Spain reported 900 new cases per day for two consecutive days.
The Foreign Minister acknowledged that many tourists did not have time to cancel or change their travel plans.
He said: "Well, I know this is a very unpleasant decision for those who are in Spain or who have been thinking about going to Spain, but we must be able to take swift and decisive action to protect Britain".
We have achieved a lot in terms of reducing the number of cases and preventing the response to the epidemic.
Shadow Health Minister Jonathan Ashworth has condemned the government's handling of the issue as unjustified.
He said that the sudden decision on Downing Street left the holidaymakers both confused and frustrated.
The Labour MP told Sophy Ridge: "Well, I understand why they made this decision, but they've made a decision like this in the last 24 hours without a chapter".
Those who are on holiday in Spain at this time will be confused and frustrated, and those who plan to go on holiday to islands such as Spain and Tenerife will be confused, not knowing whether their employers will accept their two-week quarantine.
The Chinese government is just saying that we want employers to cooperate.
The decision they made in the last 24 hours was unfounded.
According to the CDC, Salmonella is a bacterial foodborne disease that infects 1.35 million people annually, 26,500 people are hospitalized, and 420 people die.
Common symptoms of salmonella infection include diarrhea, fever and stomach ulcers, which may begin to appear within six hours to six days of exposure.
The condition usually lasts for four to seven days, and most people can recover without treatment, but some cases require hospitalization.
A couple in a dog park in California were attacked for not wearing masks at lunch.
First, there is a disgraceful disguise, and then there is a fistfight.
This is what happened to a couple in California who were having lunch with their pet dog at a dog park. One woman was furious and sprayed them with a syringe because they were not wearing a mask when they ate.
Ash O'Brien and her husband Jarett Kelley said they had lunch with their three-month-old dog at Dusty Rhodes Dog Park in San Diego on Thursday.
When the couple took off their masks and prepared to eat, an old lady who didn't know where she was coming from came over and told them to make a fool of themselves, because they weren't wearing masks, even though they were saying how to eat with them.
O'Brien allowed the woman to leave the park and stop her harassment, when the woman sprayed hot water on both of them.
The video, which was filmed by viewers, begins with Kelley fighting a woman for a spray bottle, for which he was sprayed in the face.
After the attacker left with his dog, O'Brien's cry can be heard in the background.
O'Brien posted the video on Facebook and told ABC 10 that she wanted the woman to go to jail.
Masks are mandatory throughout California, even where the air is fresh.
People can only take off their masks at prescribed times, such as when eating.
However, the state's new coronavirus pandemic prevention guidelines do not mention that bystanders can use non-lethal weapons to urge enforcement of this provision.
Even in the current era of masked humiliation, it is a crime punishable by a $1,000 fine or up to three years in state prison.
An Indore boy who sold eggs was overthrown for refusing to ride a car and was given a 100 rupee stipend for an apartment and free education
On Thursday, Paras Raykar's wagon full of eggs was reportedly overturned by municipal officials in Indore.
The story of a 13-year-old boy who sold his eggs and was hit by a pickup truck overturned by municipal officials spread on social media, and two days later, the boy who lived in Indore received support from all over the country, helping them recover from the loss of their home and restart their business.
The boy's family also said politicians including National Assembly Party chief Rahul Gandhi and Delhi Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal also contacted them and provided assistance.
The boy's family was also given an apartment and received a commitment to pay for the children's education.
On Thursday, it was reported that Paras Raykar's egg-selling pickup truck was overturned by Indore municipal officials, and the city implemented measures to switch shops from left to right as the epidemic raged.
In a video, the boy said that in the morning, officials warned him that if he didn't pay 100 rupees, he would overturn his van, and the video quickly spread on social media.
The boy said that after he refused to give 100 rupees, the officers overturned his van and smashed all the eggs.
The boy also said in the video that due to the impact of the epidemic, the daily sales of eggs have declined, and the loss of the eggs is even worse for him.
Since Thursday, the boy's family has received a large amount of support from across the country, with many providing cash or goodwill assistance.
Local people also reached out to help the family.
Former Chief Minister Digvijaya Singh has funded them and helped their children get into a school; PPP MP Ramesh Mendola has also promised to provide an apartment for boys under the Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana housing scheme.
The Indore Journalists Club also provided food and money for the boys.
The office of National Assembly Party leader Rahul Gandhi and Delhi Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal also extended their hand.
Paras's mother said: "We have the support of many people all over the country.
(MP) Ramesh Mendola Assurance Company has provided us with a suite of apartments under the Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana housing scheme.
He offered a bicycle and a grant of Rs 2,500.
Digvijaya Singh has donated Rs 10,000 and pledged to take care of the education of his two children.
Rahul Gandhi's staff also called us to help.
(Representative) A female employee of Delhi Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal also called us and helped us.
Jyothiraditya Scindia has also given support.
Interior Minister Priti Patel has asked Twitter and Instagram to explain Wiley's inappropriate content.
Since Twitter and Instagram failed to remove inappropriate content posted by Wiley in a timely manner, Priti Patel asked the two platforms to provide an explanation.
Police are currently investigating a series of anti-Semitic comments posted on social media by the Grime singer.
The Home Secretary on Sunday suggested that Wiley's anti-Semitic content was "extremely disgusting".
Twitter and Instagram have not removed the content in a timely manner, and I have asked them for a full explanation.
Social media companies must act faster to remove these shocking hate speech from their platforms.
He criticized Twitter for ignoring anti-Semitic rhetoric because Wiley's tweets were still visible 12 hours after publication.
The site subsequently deleted several tweets and blocked the 41-year-old singer's account for seven days.
Twitter has previously said it has temporarily shut down Wiley's account for violating its hate speech policy, while Facebook has said it does not allow hate speech on Instagram.
On Saturday, the Metropolitan Police Department said: "The Metropolitan Police Department is very concerned about all reports about anti-Semitism.
