Coronavirus will change the world economy forever
Just as security measures were everywhere after 9/11, virus screening could also become a part of our lives.
It is necessary to invest in the necessary infrastructure to cope with future virus detection.
If coronavirus immunization is only a short-term effect, this investment can provide security for economic operations.
During the pandemic, many economies implemented policies similar to those of Germany's Kurzarbeit subsidy.
Under this policy, workers' working hours and wages will be reduced accordingly, and underpaid wages will be partially subsidized by the government.
The initiative maintains the links between businesses and workers, thus laying a solid foundation for a rapid economic recovery.
It is also important to implement policy improvements that need to become a permanent part of economic recovery tools.
Remote work is likely to become increasingly common.
There is evidence that the home office can be as efficient as the office.
However, many companies are still reluctant to embrace the way remote work is done.
Since remote offices have been extensively tried and successfully implemented, this approach is likely to continue.
With the further development of e-commerce and the increasing adoption of remote healthcare, video conferencing, online teaching and financial technology, the epidemic crisis has also accelerated the pace of digital transformation.
Companies involved in cross-border supply chains face various shortages and bottlenecks.
