So what could be more useful for studying rocks on Mars than a piece of rock from Mars? says Professor Caroline Smith, director of the Museum of Natural History's Stone Museum.
Smith was also a member of the Mars 2020 Crime Science Team, adding that scientists believe the rocks they brought back were from Mars.
The tiny bubbles inside the limestone are exactly the same as the composition of the Martian atmosphere, so we're pretty sure the rock came from there.
The Martian meteorite is believed to have formed between 600,000 and 700,000 years ago when an asteroid or comet struck Mars and scattered debris in space.
One of the fragments swept through the solar system and eventually landed on Earth.
The diamond, now known as SAU 008, was discovered in Amman in 1999 and has been kept by the Natural History Museum ever since.
A high-precision laser emitter, named Sherloc, will be used to decipher the chemical composition of Martian rocks and determine whether they contain organic matter that could indicate that there was or is still life on Mars.
Adding a piece of SAU 008 was to ensure maximum accuracy in completing this operation.
Smith added: Well, we chose this rock especially because it fits the chemical composition and it's a very hard rock.
Some of the Martian gemstones we've found are fragile, but they're as hard as old boots.
After finding rocks of research value, the Hummingbirds will store them on the surface of Mars.
The rocks will then be sent to Mars for robot recycling and research analysis from space to Earth.
