Kenyan Garisa police arrested in connection with the shooting
Two Kenyan police officers were arrested on suspicion of causing the deaths of two people in a shootout together.
The shooting took place on Saturday at the Soko Ng'ombe market in eastern Garisa County, when the market was protesting a suspected murder case against a man.
The two victims were Aden Abdi Madobe and Muhiyadin Adow Shibin.
The Kenyan Police Authority has opened an investigation into the shooting.
The investigation process includes the examination of evidence relating to firearms.
Garisa MP Aden Duale called the incident outrageous and in a Facebook post condemned the violence of police enforcement.
Several activists have also expressed concern about the police's law enforcement actions during the pandemic.
According to Amnesty International, since March, the Kenyan police have killed 21 people due to non-compliance with the curfew and the wearing of masks.
Earlier this month, police fired tear gas in the capital, Nairobi, and arrested several participants in the annual Saba Saba march, a rally that originated 30 years ago in protest against the dictatorship of former President Daniel arap Moi.
According to the front-runner, this year's protests have become even more radical due to police crackdowns during the pandemic lockdown.
Security upgraded outside the U.S. Consulate General in Chengdu, China
On Sunday, security outside the U.S. Consulate General in Chengdu, China, was upgraded and U.S. personnel were ready to evacuate.
Three moving trucks entered the pavilion, placing uniformed police and uniformed police on both sides of the street, and setting up metal bars along the way.
The editor-in-chief of China's Xinhua Global Times tweeted that the U.S. Consulate General in Guangdong has been asked to close within 72 hours, which is Monday, July 27, at 10 a.m. (0200 GMT).
China has accused some officials of the U.S. Consulate General in Guangzhou of engaging in activities inconsistent with their identity.
Chinese Foreign Ministry spokesman Xi Jinping did not disclose any details, saying only that the activity interfered with China's internal affairs and damaged China's security interests.
On Friday, China demanded the closure of the U.S. consulate in Guangdong, a response to the U.S. request to close the Chinese consulate in Houston, Texas.
Washington demands that China shut down the consulate in Houston to protect U.S. intellectual property and the privacy of Americans.
After the Chinese consulate-general in Houston closed on Friday, a group of people who looked like U.S. officials entered the building from the back door.
Xi Jinping said on Saturday that the act was a serious violation of international law and bilateral agreements, and that China would respond, but did not specify specific measures.
The relative closure of the consulate further exacerbated tensions between the two countries over trade and industrial espionage and human rights issues.
